{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1525857503,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM4NzY3NzUyNw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2481#issuecomment-387677527"},"message":"Hm. From my point of view, the issue arises from you providing a command's result_renderer, that doesn't stick to what \"default\" is expected to look. I think, this should be used only if needed to produce that one-per-line default rendering, since your result might contain stuff, the default routine isn't able to render.\n\nThen there would be `custom_result_renderer` and `custom_result_summary_renderer` (\"tailored\" instead of \"default\") to provide something else. So, \"default\" is meant to be the command's default (as in its class attribute `result_renderer`) and *in addition* to look like described in that help message. It uses the default routine only, if that attribute isn't provided the command class.\nAt least, that's my view on it.\n\nHowever, I might be wrong about it - may be @mih has another point to make.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1525871126,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM4NzczMTY0Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2481#issuecomment-387731643"},"message":"\u003e From my point of view, the issue arises from you providing a command's result_renderer, that doesn't stick to what \"default\" is expected to look. I think, this should be used only if needed to produce that one-per-line default rendering, since your result might contain stuff, the default routine isn't able to render.\n\nAh, ok, I didn't realize that.  I thought setting `result_renderer` to \"json_pp\" or whatever was the proper way to say \"this is what the output should be by default\" and then the user could override it on the command line if they wanted.  A few existing DataLad commands do seem to use it this way.\n\nSo going back to my statement:\n\n\u003e I'd say that the problem is that we're conflating the default renderer for a command and the \"default\" output format.\n\nIt seems like the actual problem is that I'm inventing a \"default renderer for a command\" when that concept doesn't exist?  IOW, if I'm creating a command and make a custom renderer, is there any way for me to say \"this should be used unless the user specifically overrides it\"?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1525873133,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM4Nzc0MTQ5Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2481#issuecomment-387741492"},"message":"\u003e It seems like the actual problem is that I'm inventing a \"default renderer for a command\" when that concept doesn't exist?\n\nThe concept exists in a way, but I guess it's somewhat messed up in what exactly it means and how it is documented. We might want to change what default means and how it is described.\n\n\u003e IOW, if I'm creating a command and make a custom renderer, is there any way for me to say \"this should be used unless the user specifically overrides it\"?\n\nI think, this should be achieved by setting that very `result_renderer` attribute you used to \"tailored\". If you look into `_process_results()` in `interface/utils.py` it should then call that `custom_result_renderer`. (Which then of course will make the default implementation inaccessible again :-D )\n\nShort: I myself got something wrong and @mih will correct me, or we need to polish that approach indeed.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1525873725,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM4Nzc0NDUzOA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2481#issuecomment-387744538"},"message":"Actually, the more I look into it, the less sense it makes. ;-)\n\nI tend to agree to your original proposal:\n\u003e Perhaps we should give the output format a name other than \"default\", so that -f default means \"use the command's default renderer\" and -f \u003cother name\u003e means \"use the format described in the help message above\".","files":null}]}